<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#athenian-democracy">Glossary</a> · Term · government</p><h1 class="enc-title">Athenian democracy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 594–<a href="/years/338-bce">338 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Athenian democracy was the system of government that developed from around the 6th century BCE in the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and its territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, open only to free adult male citizens, probably no more than 30 percent of adults. Its development is credited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> in 594 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> in 508–507 BCE, who organised citizens into ten groups by where they lived, and Ephialtes in 462 BCE. It was direct rather than representative: citizens attended the assembly by right, and most officials and large juries were chosen by lot. Twice interrupted by oligarchic revolutions, in 411 and 404 BCE, its forms persisted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> conquered Athens in 338 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athenian-democracy.jpg" alt="Athenian democracy" width="330" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philipp Foltz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Discurso_funebre_pericles.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> was not the first polis to adopt a democratic constitution, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> points to other cities that did. Most accounts concentrate on Athens because it has the fullest record, including the only complete surviving constitution, compiled by Aristotle and his students in the 4th century BCE. Before democracy, Athens was ruled by archons and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Areopagus</a>, a council of former archons, whose members were generally aristocrats. In 621 BCE Draco replaced oral law with a written code to be enforced by a court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> later expanded the council, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boule-ancient-greece">boule</a>, to 500 members chosen by lot each year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Athenian democracy was direct rather than representative. Any adult male citizen over 20 could attend the assembly, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecclesia-ancient-greece">ekklesia</a>, by right, and was expected to. Citizens made decrees, such as declaring war or granting citizenship, elected some officials and passed laws. Speakers argued for and against a motion, and the vote was usually by show of hands. About 1,100 citizens held office each year, most chosen by lot and about 100 elected, with strict term limits. Citizens had to be at least 30 to hold office or sit on a jury.</p><p>The courts gained power over time. From 416 BCE the graphe paranomon allowed any measure of the assembly to be challenged before a jury, which could annul it and punish its proposer, and from 355 BCE political trials were held only in the courts. Women, slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metic">metics</a> and minors were excluded. In the 4th century BCE there may have been 250,000 to 300,000 people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, including some 30,000 adult male citizens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Athenian democracy had critics in its own time, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and the writer known as the Old Oligarch, who thought the common people were not the right people to rule and would make serious mistakes. The historian Samons notes that its practitioners left almost nothing but criticism of the system on a philosophical level. After the end of Athenian democracy few thought well of it. Plato and Aristotle saw it as rule by the poor, who plundered the rich, and well into the 18th century democracy was consistently condemned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Athenian democracy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Athenian democracy</b> excluded women, foreigners and tens of thousands of slaves, and the closing of Venice’s Great Council in 1297 made government the hereditary preserve of a closed patriciate.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Athenian democracy</b> survived two oligarchic coups: the regime of the Four Hundred in 411 BCE and the Thirty Tyrants, installed by victorious Sparta in 404 BCE, who put some 1,500 Athenians to death.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Athenian democracy</b> defined itself against tyranny.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
